The Library's migration tree.

It runs against the Library's own database and nothing else: `env.py` asks
`store.resolve_db()` and refuses unless `LIBRARY_DB` named the file. During the
read-only shadow there is nothing here to run, because the Library owns no schema
until the cutover hands it one.

The baseline is STAMPED, not run, on the database the cutover produces:

    LIBRARY_DB=~/.library/library.db alembic stamp head

`migrate.py` has already created every table by copying Orient's schema, so
`upgrade head` is a no-op from that point and every later revision has an ancestor
to attach to. Stage B step 5 of the cutover sequence is this command.

Autogenerate is unavailable on purpose. There is no `target_metadata`, because the
schema is defined by DDL in the revisions rather than by ORM models — `store`
holds raw SQL with explicit column lists for the reason its own docstring gives.
`alembic revision --autogenerate` would compare the database against empty
metadata and propose dropping every table in it. Write revisions by hand.

The two vec0 tables are outside this tree. `library_vectors` and `signal_vectors`
are invisible to a connection that has not loaded `sqlite-vec`, and alembic's does
not load it. They arrived through `migrate.py` at the cutover; on a fresh
database — nothing to migrate from — run `init_vectors.py` right after
`upgrade head`:

    LIBRARY_DB=~/.library/library.db .venv/bin/python -m alembic upgrade head
    LIBRARY_DB=~/.library/library.db .venv/bin/python init_vectors.py

`init_vectors.py` is idempotent (`CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`), so
running it against a database `migrate.py` already populated is a no-op.

A revision that must never run as part of an ordinary install sets a
module-level flag:

    LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY = False

`bin/install-library` backs up the vault and runs `alembic upgrade head`
itself before an install whenever the vault is behind — except when a
pending revision carries this flag, which stops it instead, naming the
file and the command to run by hand. No revision sets it today; the
Phase 3 drop stage (a drop migration plus a VACUUM, gated on a soak
window recorded in the epic, not here) will be the first, since running
it on an install's own timing is exactly the harm that stage is gated
against.

The check fails closed rather than matching only this one spelling: a
pending revision whose text mentions `LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY` anywhere stops
the auto-migrate unless every mention is exactly a module-level
`LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY = True` (optionally typed, `LIBRARY_AUTO_APPLY: bool
= True`). An indented assignment, a computed value, or any spelling of
`False` all stop it too, the same as the documented form above — so a
revision that needs to opt back in after carrying this flag has to say
`True` exactly that way, not just remove the line.

Write a column change as a batch operation:

    with op.batch_alter_table("courses") as batch:
        batch.alter_column("title", nullable=True)

`op.alter_column` on its own renders `ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN`, which
standard SQLite has no statement for. CI rejects it; the deployment machine's
much newer SQLite accepts it, so that shape passes by hand on the mini and fails
in CI. `render_as_batch` does not help — it governs how autogenerate renders a
revision, and this tree has no autogenerate.
